<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">Corrections</h1><p class="ref-lede">Every correction of fact the Institute has made to its pages, with what was wrong and what replaced it. 774 so far. When a page is corrected, the change is recorded here.</p></header>
<section class="ws-guide-sec" id="report"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Found a mistake?</h2><p>Every page ends with <b>Suggest a correction</b>, which opens the <a href="/contact?topic=correction">contact form</a> with the page filled in. Say what is wrong and, if you can, where the right version can be read. A correction that is made is listed on this page; one that is not made is answered with the reason. <a href="/method">How the Institute works</a> explains how pages are written and checked.</p></section>
<section class="ws-guide-sec" id="entries"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Encyclopedia entries: descriptions corrected (29)</h2><p>Found while checking the entries against their sources, October and November 2026.</p><ul class="corr-list"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/lorenzo-valla">Lorenzo Valla</a><span class="corr-was"><b>Was:</b> In 1440, while serving Alfonso V of Aragon at Naples, he proved</span><span class="corr-now"><b>Now:</b> In 1440, while serving Alfonso V of Aragon, then at war for the Kingdom of Naples, he proved</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/immanuel-kant">Immanuel Kant</a><span class="corr-was"><b>Was:</b> who lived and taught all his life in Königsberg in East Prussia</span><span class="corr-now"><b>Now:</b> who spent almost all his life in Königsberg in East Prussia, where he taught at the university</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/voting-rights-act-of-1965">Voting Rights Act of 1965</a><span class="corr-was"><b>Was:</b> It banned literacy tests and placed federal oversight on states with histories of discrimination, leading to a dramatic rise in Black voter registration in the South.</span><span class="corr-now"><b>Now:</b> It suspended literacy tests and placed federal oversight on the states and counties with histories of discrimination, leading to a dramatic rise in Black voter registration in the South; a nationwide ban on such tests followed in 1970.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/pazyryk-burials">Pazyryk burials</a><span class="corr-was"><b>Was:</b> excavated by Sergei Rudenko in 1929 and 1947–1949</span><span class="corr-now"><b>Now:</b> excavated in 1929 and, by Sergei Rudenko, in 1947–1949</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/polynesians">Polynesians</a><span class="corr-was"><b>Was:</b> they reached Tonga and Samoa around 800 BCE</span><span class="corr-now"><b>Now:</b> they reached Tonga and Samoa around 900–800 BCE</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/self-strengthening-movement">Self-Strengthening Movement</a><span class="corr-was"><b>Was:</b> 1860s–1880s</span><span class="corr-now"><b>Now:</b> 1861–1895</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/self-strengthening-movement">Self-Strengthening Movement</a><span class="corr-was"><b>Was:</b> from the 1860s to the 1880s</span><span class="corr-now"><b>Now:</b> from 1861 to 1895</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/chonin">Chōnin</a><span class="corr-was"><b>Was:</b> Officially ranked below samurai and peasants in the Tokugawa Shogunate's social hierarchy, they grew wealthy through commerce in cities such as Edo and Osaka.</span><span class="corr-now"><b>Now:</b> Confucian theory placed them below the samurai and the peasants, though historians now hold that in law the commoners were classed, not ranked, beneath the samurai. They grew wealthy through commerce in cities such as Edo and Osaka.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/faxian">Faxian</a><span class="corr-was"><b>Was:</b> 399–414 CE in India</span><span class="corr-now"><b>Now:</b> fl. 399–414</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/faxian">Faxian</a><span class="corr-was"><b>Was:</b> staying there during 399–414 CE.</span><span class="corr-now"><b>Now:</b> a journey that lasted from 399 to 412, about ten years of it in India.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/xiang-jun">Xiang jun</a><span class="corr-was"><b>Was:</b> The xiang jun was the professional standing army of the Song Dynasty. It was very large, with estimates of over one million men under arms at its peak. However, it was notoriously ill-disciplined</span><span class="corr-now"><b>Now:</b> The xiang jun were the prefectural armies of the Song Dynasty: professional troops stationed in the provinces and used largely for labour and transport, while the imperial army (jinjun) was the fighting core. At its peak the whole Song army numbered over one million men, notoriously ill-disciplined</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/millet-system">Millet system</a><span class="corr-was"><b>Was:</b> The system helped the Ottomans govern a diverse population.</span><span class="corr-now"><b>Now:</b> The system helped the Ottomans govern a diverse population. Historians such as Benjamin Braude argue that the formal system of officially recognised millets took shape only in the 18th and 19th centuries; earlier arrangements were looser and varied from place to place.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/benin-bronzes">Benin Bronzes</a><span class="corr-was"><b>Was:</b> The site dates the tradition to 1485–1897, from early contact with the Portuguese to the British expedition that looted them from Benin City.</span><span class="corr-now"><b>Now:</b> The oldest date from the 14th century and most from the 15th and 16th, long before the British expedition that looted them from Benin City in 1897; late-19th-century scholars wrongly credited the craft to contact with the Portuguese.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/benin-bronzes">Benin Bronzes</a><span class="corr-was"><b>Was:</b> 1485–1897</span><span class="corr-now"><b>Now:</b> 14th century–1897</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a><span class="corr-was"><b>Was:</b> A mamluk was an enslaved soldier, usually of Turkic or Caucasian origin, bought and trained for military service in Islamic states.</span><span class="corr-now"><b>Now:</b> A mamluk was a soldier of slave origin, usually Turkic or Caucasian, bought as a boy and trained for military service in Islamic states, and often freed when his training ended.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/caravel">Caravel</a><span class="corr-was"><b>Was:</b> A caravel was a small, highly manoeuvrable sailing ship developed by the Portuguese in the fifteenth century, often with lateen sails that allowed it to sail close to the wind. It was used in voyages of exploration along the African coast.</span><span class="corr-now"><b>Now:</b> A caravel was a small, highly manoeuvrable sailing ship, often with lateen sails that allowed it to sail close to the wind. It developed from the fishing craft of Galicia, Portugal and Atlantic Andalusia, where the word is first recorded in Portuguese in 1255, and in the fifteenth century the Portuguese made it the ship of their voyages of exploration along the African coast.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">Gunpowder</a><span class="corr-was"><b>Was:</b> and the military consequences for states such as the Songhai Empire that faced gunpowder armies.</span><span class="corr-now"><b>Now:</b> and armies equipped with it overcame states that lacked it, such as the Songhai Empire.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/maxim-gun">Maxim gun</a><span class="corr-was"><b>Was:</b> It gave European colonial forces overwhelming firepower over African armies. British forces used it in the conquest of the Sokoto Caliphate, where defenders had no answer to its fire.</span><span class="corr-now"><b>Now:</b> Colonial powers used it heavily in the Scramble for Africa, and British forces used it in the conquest of the Sokoto Caliphate. Its destructive power has often been embellished by popular myth: modern accounts find it effective in pitched battles, as at Omdurman, but owing much of its significance to its psychological impact.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/steamship">Steamship</a><span class="corr-was"><b>Was:</b> In the nineteenth century steam-powered warships gave Western powers decisive naval superiority. The Qing Empire and Tokugawa Shogunate profiles describe how they were confronted with such ships.</span><span class="corr-now"><b>Now:</b> In the nineteenth century steam warships carried Western power to East Asia: a British fleet that included the iron steamer Nemesis overcame the Qing Empire's coastal defences in the First Opium War of 1839–1842, and in 1853 Commodore Matthew Perry's squadron, which included steam-powered warships, entered Edo Bay and demanded that the Tokugawa Shogunate open Japan's ports.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/royal-road">Royal Road</a><span class="corr-was"><b>Was:</b> Royal couriers using fresh horses could carry messages across it in around seven days.</span><span class="corr-now"><b>Now:</b> Mounted royal couriers, changing horses at the stations, were supposed to cover it in nine days; the journey took ninety days on foot.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/griots">Griots</a><span class="corr-was"><b>Was:</b> Griots are hereditary oral historians, praise-singers and musicians of West Africa who preserve genealogies and historical traditions. They held a recognised place in the social structure of the region's states. In the Jolof Empire they formed part of the caste-based social order and maintained the memory of rulers and lineages.</span><span class="corr-now"><b>Now:</b> Griots are the hereditary oral historians, praise singers, storytellers and musicians of West Africa, who preserve genealogies and historical traditions; among the Mande peoples they are the jeli or jali. Historically an endogamous caste, they served higher-ranking families of warrior kings or emperors, advising them and mediating in disputes, yet were held apart: in the worst case they were buried in trees, not the ground, to avoid polluting the earth. In the Jolof Empire they were part of the caste-based social order. The Epic of Sundiata names Balla Fasséké as the griot given to Sundiata Keita as adviser, the founder of the Kouyaté line of griots, which still exists.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/caravel">Caravel</a><span class="corr-was"><b>Was:</b> It developed from the fishing craft of Galicia, Portugal and Atlantic Andalusia, where the word is first recorded in Portuguese in 1255, and in the fifteenth century</span><span class="corr-now"><b>Now:</b> It developed from the fishing boats of Portugal and the Atlantic coast of Spain; the word is first recorded in Portuguese in 1255, and in the fifteenth century</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/dinavar">Dinavar</a><span class="corr-was"><b>Was:</b> the polymath Abu Hanifa al-Dinawari</span><span class="corr-now"><b>Now:</b> the polymath al-Dinawari</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/sheikh-ul-islam">Sheikh ul-Islam</a><span class="corr-was"><b>Was:</b> Sheikh ul-Islam was the title of the chief religious authority in the Ottoman Empire, who headed the learned hierarchy of Islamic scholars. The holder issued legal opinions (fatwas) on matters of law and state policy. Accounts of the Ottoman Empire treat the office as an important part of Ottoman governance, linking the state to Sunni religious legitimacy.</span><span class="corr-now"><b>Now:</b> Sheikh ul-Islam, or Shaykh al-Islam ('elder of Islam'), was an honorific title for outstanding scholars of the Islamic sciences, which first appeared in Khurasan in the 10th century. In Syria and Egypt it was an informal title for jurists whose fatwas were especially influential, while under the Ilkhans, the Delhi Sultanate and the Timurids rulers conferred it on high-ranking ulama. In the Ottoman Empire it came to designate the chief mufti, head of a hierarchy of state-appointed ulama, who issued fatwas, advised the sultan, appointed judges and confirmed new sultans. The office was abolished with the Caliphate in 1924 and replaced by the Presidency of Religious Affairs.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/mani">Mani</a><span class="corr-was"><b>Was:</b> Mani was a Persian prophet of the third century CE and founder of Manichaeism. According to the Aksumite Empire's profile, he described Aksum as one of the four great powers of the world, alongside Rome, Persia and China. His statement is often cited as evidence of Aksum's international standing.</span><span class="corr-now"><b>Now:</b> Mani was a prophet born in 216 in Mesopotamia, near the Persian capital Ctesiphon, who from about 240 preached the religion later called Manichaeism. He taught that the world is a mixture of light and darkness and that souls, sparks of light, can be freed through knowledge and discipline, and he presented himself as the last in a line of prophets after the Buddha, Zoroaster and Jesus. He died in prison under the Sasanian king Bahram I, around 274 to 277, and his faith spread from Roman North Africa to China. He described Aksum as one of the four great powers of the world, alongside Rome, Persia and China.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/stoicism">Stoicism</a><span class="corr-was"><b>Was:</b> Taken up by Romans such as Seneca, Epictetus</span><span class="corr-now"><b>Now:</b> Taken up by Romans such as Seneca the Younger, Epictetus</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/albertino-mussato">Albertino Mussato</a><span class="corr-was"><b>Was:</b> written in the style of Seneca</span><span class="corr-now"><b>Now:</b> written in the style of Seneca the Younger</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/erfurt-division">Erfurt Division</a><span class="corr-was"><b>Was:</b> in November 1572. the Weimar line</span><span class="corr-now"><b>Now:</b> in November 1572. The Weimar line</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/gaza-empire">Gaza Empire</a><span class="corr-was"><b>Was:</b> was an Nguni state</span><span class="corr-now"><b>Now:</b> was a Nguni state</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="ws-guide-sec" id="pictures"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Pictures that showed something else (96)</h2><p>An audit in November 2026 compared every entry's picture, dates and map point with its source and found pictures taken from a namesake's article. Each was removed; some entries have since been given the right picture.</p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/ferns">Ferns</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed the plant fern (the article &quot;Fern&quot;), not the village in County Wexford.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/panduranga">Panduranga</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed the Hindu god Vithoba, also called Panduranga, not the region of Champa.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/phaenomena">Phaenomena</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed a struck match (the article &quot;Phenomenon&quot;), not the poem of Aratus.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/rijali">Rijali</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed hadith books (the article on rijal, biographical evaluation), not the imam of Hitu.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/erzberg">Erzberg</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed the Hochtor and Ödstein group of the Ennstal Alps, not the Erzberg.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/moch-couoh">Moch Couoh</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed Cortés scuttling his ships (1519), not the lord of Champotón.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/pravarapura">Pravarapura</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed a Sufi shrine in Wardha district, not the Vakataka capital.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/rasamanjari">Rasamanjari</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed a tambura (the article &quot;Rasikapriya&quot;, another work), not the poem of Bhanudatta.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/the-deluge">The Deluge</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed a painting of the biblical flood, not the Swedish invasion of Poland-Lithuania.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/taehak">Taehak</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed the Myeongnyundang of the Joseon Sungkyunkwan, not the Goguryeo academy.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/earl-of-kildare">Earl of Kildare</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed the coronet of a duke (the article &quot;Duke of Leinster&quot;), not an earl.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/county-palatine-of-saxony">County Palatine of Saxony</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed the arms of Augustus III as imperial vicar, another office.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/lake-miramar">Lake Miramar</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed Miramar Reservoir in San Diego, not the lake in Chiapas.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/hungarian-red-army">Hungarian Red Army</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed the war flag of present-day Hungary, not the army of 1919.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-alliance">National Liberation Alliance</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed a rally of the Thai People's Alliance for Democracy, not the Brazilian front of 1935.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/kanye">Kanye</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed the American rapper Kanye West, not the village in Botswana.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/turkmenbasy">Türkmenbaşy</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed Saparmurat Niyazov, after whom the city was named.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/valuk">Valuk</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed a modern armoured personnel carrier named Valuk, not the Carantanian ruler.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/gajayana">Gajayana</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed an Indonesian train named Gajayana, not the king of Kanjuruhan.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/taksony">Taksony</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed the present-day village of Taksony, not the Grand Prince.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/bohechio">Bohechío</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed the present-day town of Bohechío, not the cacique of Jaragua.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/galeshewe">Galeshewe</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed the township named after him, not the Batlhaping chief.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/sultan-kudarat">Sultan Kudarat</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed the capitol of the province named after him, not the sultan.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/duden">Duden</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed the German dictionary Duden, not the Mongol prince.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/kurunta">Kurunta</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed a stag rhyton of the god Kurunta, not the Hittite prince.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/al-mustansir-billah">Al-Mustansir Billah</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed the Fatimid caliph al-Mustansir (1029-1094), not the Abbasid caliph.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/al-qasim-ibn-muhammad">Al-Qasim ibn Muhammad</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed Mecca, from the article on an early Muslim namesake, not the Zaydi imam.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/abdul-rauf-khan">Abdul Rauf Khan</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed a present-day namesake born in 1969, not the founder of Savanur.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/george-of-saxony">George of Saxony</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed King George of Saxony (1832-1904), not Duke George the Bearded.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-zaki">Ibrahim Zaki</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed the Maldivian envoy Ibrahim Hussain Zaki, not the Fulani commander.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/jacob-sturm">Jacob Sturm</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed Jacob Sturm the engraver (1771-1848), not the statesman of Strasbourg.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/liu-kun">Liu Kun</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed a present-day namesake, not the Western Jin general.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/malik-sy">Malik Sy</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed El Hadji Malick Sy (1855-1922), not the founder of Bundu.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/zulfiqar-khan">Zulfiqar Khan</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed a present-day musician, not the Mughal general.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/vimal-shah">Vimal Shah</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed a present-day namesake, not the Jain minister.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/liu-fang">Liu Fang</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed the pipa player Liu Fang, not the Sui general.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/liu-jia">Liu Jia</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed a present-day namesake, not the Han general.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/james-heane">James Heane</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed a First World War brigadier of the name, not the Parliamentarian officer.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/jean-lamour">Jean Lamour</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed the French politician Jean-François Lamour, not the ironworker of Nancy.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/khusrau-shah">Khusrau Shah</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed Khusrau Shah of the Justanids, not the Ghaznavid sultan.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/jahangir-khan">Jahangir Khan</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed the squash player Jahangir Khan, not the khan of the Kazakhs.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/antonio-zapata">Antonio Zapata</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed Cardinal Antonio Zapata y Cisneros, not the federalist of Tamaulipas.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/liu-xijun">Liu Xijun</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed a present-day singer, not the Han princess.</span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/pinnes">Pinnes</a><span class="corr-was">The picture showed a map of the Ardiaean kingdom of another Pinnes, not the Pannonian leader.</span></li><li><a href="/encyc