<section class="ws-guide-sec" id="profiles"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Empire profiles: the fact-check of October 2026 (649 corrections in 94 profiles)</h2><p>All 94 empire profiles were read against standard histories in October 2026. Each line gives the earlier wording, what replaced it and why. Open a profile to see its corrections.</p><details class="corr-prof"><summary>Abbasid Caliphate <span class="corr-n">6</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Read the profile of Abbasid Caliphate →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Turkic ghilman army created by al-Mu'tasim in 833&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;built up by al-Mu'tasim&quot; (he began recruiting Turkic slave soldiers as a prince, well before his accession in 833).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Barmakids &quot;served under al-Mansur and Harun al-Rashid&quot; as viziers <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> served the early caliphs and peaked as viziers under Harun until their fall in 803 (their vizierate proper belongs to Harun's reign).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Al-Khwarizmi's &quot;invention of algebra&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> the treatise that gave algebra its name, dated c. 820 (exact date uncertain).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Hulagu's forces breached the walls on 10 February 1258&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;took the city in February 1258&quot; (walls were breached earlier in the month; the surrender/entry date is debated).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Baghdad &quot;the largest city in the world&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;one of the largest&quot; (contested claim); Ctesiphon &quot;near&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;not far from&quot; (c. 30 km away); &quot;zero political authority&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;little or no&quot;.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Abu Muslim &quot;a Persian commander&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;of obscure but probably Persian origin&quot; (his origins are disputed).</span></li></ul></details>
<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Achaemenid Empire <span class="corr-n">6</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Read the profile of Achaemenid Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;permanent loss of Egypt in 404 BCE&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> loss of Egypt for six decades, reconquered by Artaxerxes III in 343 BCE (Egypt was recovered before Alexander; the original also contradicted its own &quot;61 years&quot;).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Median inheritance &quot;modern Iran, Azerbaijan, and much of Anatolia&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;the Iranian plateau to eastern Anatolia as far as the Halys&quot; (Media never held much of Anatolia; Lydia held the west).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;encompassing an estimated 44 percent of the world's population&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;a large proportion ... (modern estimates vary widely)&quot; (popular figure without sound basis).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;great revolts of 522–520 BCE&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;revolts that followed his accession in 522 BCE&quot; (main revolts 522–521; chronology varies).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Lydia campaign &quot;Battle of Thymbra in 546 BCE&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;traditionally at the Battle of Thymbra ... around 547–546 BCE&quot; (date and battle name uncertain).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Persepolis burned &quot;early 330&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;330 BCE&quot; (burning usually dated to spring/May 330); Artaxerxes III &quot;assassination&quot; specified as poisoning.</span></li></ul></details>
<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Adal Sultanate <span class="corr-n">7</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/adal-sultanate">Read the profile of Adal Sultanate →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">Ahmad's figurehead sultans &quot;first Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad, then his brother Umar Din&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Ahmad opposed and killed Abu Bakr (c. 1525) and installed Umar Din as figurehead (Abu Bakr was a rival, not a puppet).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Monasteries destroyed &quot;at Debre Damo, Lalibela, and Lake Tana&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;Debre Libanos and Atronsa Maryam and the island monasteries of Lake Tana&quot; (Ahmad failed to take Debre Damo; Lalibela's churches survived).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Decline listed &quot;execution of Sultan Muhammad V in 1577&quot; and separately &quot;death of Muhammad ibn Nasir at the Battle of Webi River&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> one event: Sarsa Dengel defeated and executed Sultan Muhammad ibn Nasir near the Webi in 1576; withdrawal to Aussa in 1577.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Sabr ad-Din reconstituted the state &quot;at Zeila&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> seat at Dakkar with Zeila as port; capital &quot;shifted from Zeila to Dakar&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> at Dakkar until moved to Harar in 1520 by Abu Bakr; Harar capital &quot;from the mid-fifteenth century&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;early sixteenth century&quot;.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Sa'ad ad-Din II &quot;captured and executed by Dawit I around 1410&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> defeated and killed near Zeila by Ethiopian forces, date given variously c. 1403–1415 (disputed).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Within weeks, three-quarters of Ethiopia had been liberated&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> highlands recovered &quot;within a few years&quot;.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Ethiopian–Adal War &quot;1527–1543&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> 1529–1543 in impact (campaign years 1527–1543 kept in socioeconomy for raids); specific troop numbers at Shimbra Kure (5,000–6,000 vs 15,000–20,000) removed as uncertain <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;a much larger Ethiopian force&quot;. Harar &quot;one of the holiest cities in Islam&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;a renowned centre of Islamic learning&quot;.</span></li></ul></details>
<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Afsharid Empire <span class="corr-n">8</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/afsharid-empire">Read the profile of Afsharid Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">Isfahan liberated &quot;after eight years of occupation&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;seven years&quot; (Afghans held it 1722–1729).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Reza Qoli blinded &quot;in 1741&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;in 1742&quot; (the assassination attempt was 1741; the blinding followed in 1742).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Ahmad Shah founded the Durrani Empire &quot;within weeks of Nader's death&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;within months&quot; (chosen at Kandahar in autumn 1747).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">By 1736 Nader &quot;had already assembled an army of approximately 375,000 men&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;a large, battle-hardened army&quot; (the 375,000 figure refers, if at all, to his later peak, not 1736).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Indian campaign order &quot;crossing the Khyber in December 1738, sweeping through Peshawar and Lahore, crossing the Indus&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Khyber (late 1738), Peshawar, Indus, Lahore, Karnal, Delhi (March 1739).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Ja'fari proposal &quot;failed to gain acceptance from the Shi'a clergy and was quietly abandoned&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> pursued via the Najaf conference (1743), never accepted by the Ottomans, lapsed at his death.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Empire contracted to a rump &quot;within weeks&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;within a few years&quot;; &quot;the world's most powerful state&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;one of the most powerful states of its age&quot;; Ahmad Shah &quot;commander and treasurer&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> commander of the Abdali contingent; &quot;jezzailchis ... mounted infantry&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> jazayerchi musketeers (the mounted-infantry claim is not well established).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Nader's 1736 name &quot;Nader Qoli&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;Tahmasp Qoli Khan&quot; (the title he bore in Tahmasp II's service); Mughal army &quot;six times larger in under three hours&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;several times larger in a few hours&quot;; &quot;700 million rupees&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;hundreds of millions of rupees&quot; (estimates vary).</span></li></ul></details>
<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Akkadian Empire <span class="corr-n">6</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Read the profile of Akkadian Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">Sargon &quot;washing his weapons in the sea&quot; placed at the Mediterranean <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> refers to the Lower Sea (Persian Gulf) after conquering Sumer; western campaigns described as reaching the Cedar Forest and Silver Mountains towards the Mediterranean.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;installed Akkadian officials across more than 65 cities and established postal ... systems&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> officials placed in the conquered cities (the figure and a postal system are not securely attested); &quot;postal system&quot; also removed from governance.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;revolts that plagued the later reigns of Sargon, Rimush, and Manishtushu&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Sargon, Rimush and Naram-Sin (the Great Revolt was under Naram-Sin; Manishtushu's reign is less associated with revolt).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;city of Akkad itself was destroyed and never reoccupied&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;declined into obscurity&quot; (Akkad is still mentioned in later texts; its destruction is not established).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Akkadian as lingua franca &quot;for nearly two millennia&quot; used &quot;between Babylon and Assyria&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> in its Babylonian form for much of the second millennium BCE; Macedonian empire removed from list of heirs.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Anatolia campaigns <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> attributed to later legend; Enheduanna &quot;the first named author&quot; hedged (&quot;often described as&quot;); &quot;merchant guilds&quot; removed (anachronistic).</span></li></ul></details>
