<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Aztec Empire <span class="corr-n">8</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/aztec-empire">Read the profile of Aztec Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">Ahuitzotl's conquests reached &quot;deep into what is now Guatemala&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> reached Xoconochco (Soconusco) near the present Guatemalan border (Aztec control did not extend deep into Guatemala).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">calpixque described as &quot;regional governors&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> tribute stewards (their actual role).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Moctezuma II credited with creating &quot;38 provincial divisions&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> the 38 tribute provinces are those recorded in the Codex Mendoza; Moctezuma's reforms described as tightening central control (attribution of the 38-province scheme to him is unfounded).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">final-siege allies listed as Tlaxcalans, Texcocans, Totonacs &quot;subjected to Aztec tribute&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Tlaxcalans, Huexotzincas, Chalcans, Texcocan factions; Tlaxcala and Huexotzinco were independent enemies, not tributaries.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">smallpox &quot;killed an estimated 50 percent&quot; of the city <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;a large share&quot; (figure is not well established); 100,000-strong allied army framed as &quot;by some estimates&quot;.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Acamapichtli dates c. 1376 <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> c. 1375 (conventional dating).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;most rapid in recorded imperial history&quot; / &quot;fastest collapse in the historical record&quot