<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Han Dynasty <span class="corr-n">6</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/han-dynasty">Read the profile of Han Dynasty →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">Liu Bang &quot;proclaimed the Han dynasty in 206 BCE, taking the imperial title&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> made King of Han in 206 BCE; proclaimed emperor in 202 BCE after Gaixia</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Taxes reduced &quot;to one-thirtieth&quot; by Gaozu <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Gaozu set one-fifteenth; later reduced to one-thirtieth (under Emperor Jing)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Emperor Wu's campaigns &quot;tripled the empire's territorial extent&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;vastly enlarged the empire&quot; (figure unsupported)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Silk &quot;reaching Rome through the Central Asian trade routes&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> through Parthian and other intermediaries (no direct trade); &quot;first sustained transcontinental exchange ... in human history&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;an unprecedented sustained exchange ... across Eurasia&quot;</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Han legal code ... a more systematic framework than the Qin&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Han law built on Qin statutes, moderated by reforms such as Emperor Wen's abolition of mutilating punishments (167 BCE); &quot;censorate&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Imperial Censor and provincial inspectors (the censorate as a formal body is later)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Population &quot;approximately 60 million by the first century CE&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→