<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Holy Roman Empire <span class="corr-n">6</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Read the profile of Holy Roman Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;This enforced pluralism created the ... environment from which both the Scientific Revolution and the Reformation emerged&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> fragmentation allowed the Reformation (begun 1517) to survive (the Reformation predates the 1555 and 1648 settlements, so it could not have emerged from them)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Peace of Augsburg and Westphalia both &quot;established&quot; cuius regio <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Augsburg (Catholic/Lutheran choice) and Westphalia (extended to Calvinism)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Westphalia recognised princes' right &quot;to conduct their own foreign policy&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;to conclude alliances with foreign powers&quot; (the actual ius foederis, with limits)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Otto I crowned &quot;Holy Roman Emperor&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;emperor&quot; (the &quot;Holy Roman&quot; title is later)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Golden Bull codified election of &quot;the emperor&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;the King of the Romans&quot; (the electors chose the king; imperial coronation followed)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Hanseatic cities connected northern markets &quot;to Mediterranean&quot; networks <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> North Sea and Baltic to wider European markets</span></li></ul></details>
