<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Jolof Empire <span class="corr-n">6</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/jolof-empire">Read the profile of Jolof Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">Jolof arose &quot;in the aftermath of the fragmentation of the Mali Empire&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> on Mali's western margins as its hold on the far west loosened (Mali was still near its height in the mid-fourteenth century)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Wolof-speaking states such as Waalo, Cayor, Baol, and Sine&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Wolof states Waalo, Cayor, Baol plus the Serer kingdoms of Sine and Saloum (Sine is Serer, not Wolof)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;By the late sixteenth century, Jolof had dissolved into a collection of independent states&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> vassals became independent, but Jolof survived as an inland kingdom until absorbed into French Senegal in 1890</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;External pressures were less significant&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> removed; added Koli Tenguella's Fula invasions as an external strain</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">traded goods &quot;Salt, gold, hides, and slaves&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> horses, cloth, hides and captives (horses-for-slaves exchange is the best-attested trade)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;germi&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;garmi&quot;; &quot;bourba&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;buurba&quot; (standard spellings)</span></li></ul></details>
