<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Kanem-Bornu Empire <span class="corr-n">4</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/kanem-bornu-empire">Read the profile of Kanem-Bornu Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Although Kanem–Bornu initially resisted, it eventually succumbed&quot; to the Fulani jihad <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Bornu lost Ngazargamu in 1808 but survived under al-Kanemi, who repelled the jihadists</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;end of its political independence occurred in 1893 with the defeat of its final rulers ... particularly by French forces&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> conquered in 1893 by Rabih az-Zubayr; French killed Rabih in 1900 and the territory was partitioned among Britain, France and Germany</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">consolidation &quot;traditionally associated with the semi-legendary Sayf ibn Dhi Yazan&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Sayf was the dynasty's claimed legendary ancestor; Duguwa dynasty preceded the Sayfawa</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">economic strain from &quot;shifting global trade patterns favoring Atlantic routes&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> softened; trans-Saharan trade continued but lost relative importance</span></li></ul></details>
<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Khazar Khaganate <span class="corr-n">5</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/khazar-khaganate">Read the profile of Khazar Khaganate →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">decline &quot;occurred between the late ninth and mid-tenth centuries&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;unfolded over the tenth century&quot; (the decisive Rus campaigns came in the 960s, after the stated range)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">conversion to Judaism &quot;was a strategic decision that reinforced neutrality&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;is often interpreted as&quot; such (motive is a scholarly interpretation, not established fact)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;The Khazars were instrumental in the dissemination of silver dirhams&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;helping to channel&quot; (Volga Bulgaria and other intermediaries were equally important)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">ruling &quot;Ashina lineage&quot; stated as fact <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;probably a branch of the Ashina clan&quot; (debated)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">wars with &quot;the Umayyad and Abbasid Caliphates&quot; &quot;successfully resisted&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Umayyad wars specified, including the temporary submission forced by Marwan in 737</span></li></ul></details>
<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Khmer Empire <span class="corr-n">4</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/khmer-empire">Read the profile of Khmer Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">Jayavarman II &quot;declaring himself a universal monarch (devaraja)&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> universal monarch (chakravartin) while inaugurating the devaraja cult (the two terms are distinct)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">hydraulic system &quot;enabling multiple annual rice harvests&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;helping to secure harvests&quot; (multiple cropping from the barays is debated)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;the sack of Angkor in 1431&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;the capture of Angkor traditionally dated to 1431&quot; (the date is traditional and debated)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Jayavarman VII ... reorganize the military and fortify capital defenses&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> expelled the Cham, walled Angkor Thom and subjugated Champa in 1203</span></li></ul></details>
<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Khwarazmian Empire <span class="corr-n">4</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/khwarazmian-empire">Read the profile of Khwarazmian Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Ala ad-Din Muhammad II ordered the execution of Mongol envoys and merchants&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> the merchants were massacred by the governor of Otrar; the shah then executed the envoy sent to demand redress (distinguishes the two acts)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;remnants of Khwarazmian forces continued to operate in exile for a short period&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Jalal ad-Din fought on until his death in 1231 (a decade, not a short period)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">governance &quot;a centralized monarchy&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> monarchy undermined by Terken Khatun's parallel court (centralisation was notably weak)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Anushteginid dynasty, founded by Anushtegin&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> named after Anushtegin; effective independence came under Atsiz and Tekish</span></li></ul></details>
<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Kongo Empire <span class="corr-n">4</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/kongo-empire">Read the profile of Kongo Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Efforts at reunification were largely unsuccessful&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Pedro IV reoccupied São Salvador and nominally reunified the kingdom in 1709, though real power stayed with provincial lords</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;By the eighteenth century, Kongo had effectively disintegrated into smaller polities&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> fragmented after Mbwila but survived as a diminished monarchy into the early twentieth century</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;limited use of cavalry due to environmental constraints&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> no cavalry (tsetse fly made horses impractical)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Kongo &quot;around the lower Congo River&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;south of the lower Congo River&quot; (core territory lay south of the river)</span></li></ul></details>
<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Kushite Empire <span class="corr-n">4</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/kushite-empire">Read the profile of Kushite Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;After the loss of Egypt ... the center of power shifted permanently southward to Meroë&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> power was concentrated at Napata first; the move to Meroë was gradual, after Psamtik II's raid c. 593 BCE, completed by c. 300 BCE</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Kush &quot;initially centered at Kerma around 2500 BCE&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> heir to the earlier Kerma culture (Kerma was a separate earlier state, conquered by Egypt c. 1500 BCE)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">kandakes described as &quot;queen mothers&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> queen mothers had a role in succession, and several queens titled kandake ruled in their own right</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Aksum &quot;invaded and weakened Kushite political structures&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Ezana's inscription records a campaign against the Noba and Kasu when Meroë was apparently already in decline</span></li></ul></details>
<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Luba Empire <span class="corr-n">7</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/luba-empire">Read the profile of Luba Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;balopwe monarch&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;mulopwe&quot; (mulopwe is the king's title; balopwe is the plural for royals; the sacred-kingship concept is bulopwe).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Bushime River&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;Bushimaie River&quot; (spelling of the river).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;incursions by Ovimbundu slave hunters and the Portuguese&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Swahili-Arab and Nyamwezi traders (incl. Msiri) from the east and Ovimbundu/Chokwe caravans from the west (no significant Portuguese incursion into Luba lands).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Belgian colonial forces, arriving after 1893&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;Congo Free State forces arrived in the 1890s&quot; (the territory was Leopold II's Free State, not Belgian, until 1908).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;By 1917, the final pre-colonial Mulopwe existed only as a client chief&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Kasongo Nyembo resisted until his capture in 1917, after which Luba rulers were colonial chiefs.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">civil war between &quot;Kasongo Nyembo and Kumwimbe Tshimbu&quot; splitting into &quot;Kasongo Luba and Kabongo Luba&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> rephrased cautiously as division between the houses of Kasongo Nyembo and Kabongo; &quot;late 19th century&quot; death of Ilunga Kabale <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;around 1870&quot; (consistent with his c. 1840–1870 reign given in the military section).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Around 1585&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;Traditionally around 1585&quot; (date derives from oral tradition).</span></li></ul></details>
<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Lunda Empire <span class="corr-n">8</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/lunda-empire">Read the profile of Lunda Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">royal title &quot;Mwaantaanga&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;Mwaant Yaav (Mwata Yamvo)&quot; (the Lunda kingship title; &quot;Mwaantaanga&quot; is not attested).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;originated in the late fifteenth century&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;took shape in the sixteenth and early seventeenth centuries&quot; (the Lunda state is generally dated to c. 1600–1665).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Mwaant Yaav I (c. 1665–1700)&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;the first Mwaant Yaav, traditionally Naweej I&quot; in the later seventeenth century (Mwaant Yaav is the title, not a personal name; years removed as uncertain).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;cavalry was occasionally employed in flatter terrains&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> removed (horses could not survive in the tsetse zone; there was no Lunda cavalry).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">slave trade &quot;remained secondary&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;deeply involved&quot; via Angolan intermediaries (the Lunda were a major supplier to the Atlantic trade in the 18th–19th centuries).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Chokwe and Lunda offshoot states&quot; emulating the Lunda <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Kazembe, Luvale, Ndembu; Chokwe recast as the rising rival that overran the capital in the mid-1880s.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Belgian and Portuguese incursions&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> partition between the Congo Free State, Portuguese Angola and British Northern Rhodesia.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">location &quot;southeastern DRC&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;south-western DRC, north-eastern Angola and north-western Zambia&quot;.</span></li></ul></details>
<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Macedonian Empire <span class="corr-n">8</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/macedonian-empire">Read the profile of Macedonian Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Cities such as Alexandria, Antioch, and Pergamon&quot; as products of the empire <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Antioch identified as a later successor foundation and Pergamon as an older city (both rose under the successor kingdoms, not under Alexander).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;fielding forces of approximately 40,000 infantry&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;some 30,000 to 40,000 infantry and about 5,000 cavalry&quot; (ancient figures for the 334 BCE invasion force range from c. 30,000 to 43,000).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">satraps &quot;reporting directly to the emperor&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;answered to the king&quot; (Macedonian rulers were kings).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Macedon's metals &quot;particularly iron and gold&quot; via trade <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> its own timber and the gold and silver mines of Mount Pangaion seized by Philip.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Diadochi wars &quot;323–281 BCE&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;322–281 BCE&quot; (the first war began in 322).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Peloponnesian League and the fractious alliances&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> hegemony struggles among Sparta, Athens and Thebes.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;By the 2nd century BCE ... absorbed&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Pydna 168 BCE and Roman province 146 BCE.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;vulnerable to neighboring Greek city-states&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> also Illyrian and Thracian raids.</span></li></ul></details>
<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Majapahit Empire <span class="corr-n">7</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/majapahit-empire">Read the profile of Majapahit Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;leveraged alliances with Mongol forces sent by Kublai Khan to suppress regional rivals&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> clarified that the Mongols came to punish Kertanegara and Wijaya used them against Jayakatwang of Kediri before expelling them.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;decline began in the late 14th century&quot; kept but anchored to Hayam Wuruk's death (1389); &quot;By the early 16th century, the capital at Trowulan lost prominence&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> traditional fall date 1478 with a rump court until Demak's conquest c. 1527.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;resist external incursions from ... Chinese fleets&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> removed (Majapahit had tributary relations with Ming China, not naval war against it).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;elite cavalry&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> removed/qualified (horses and elephants were elite/ceremonial, not a cavalry arm).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;terraced paddies&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> irrigated wet-rice in the river valleys (Brantas valley irrigation, not terracing, was the base).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;devaraja ideology&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Hindu-Buddhist ideals of divine kingship (the term devaraja is chiefly associated with Angkor).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Gajah Mada as &quot;prime minister and military strategist during Hayam Wuruk's reign&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> campaigns directed by Gajah Mada (he was chief minister from the 1330s, under Tribhuwana as well).</span></li></ul></details>
<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Mali Empire <span class="corr-n">9</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/mali-empire">Read the profile of Mali Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Mali ... literally 'where the king lives'&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;traditionally interpreted as&quot; (the etymology is traditional, not certain).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Following the conquest of Koumbi Saleh in 1240&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> subjugation of the Ghanaian heartland &quot;traditionally dated to 1240&quot; (date from tradition).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;consolidate twelve kingdoms&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;unite the Manding chiefdoms&quot; (the &quot;twelve&quot; is a traditional formula).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Bambuk, Bondu, and Bure&quot; goldfields <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;Bambuk and Bure&quot; (Bondu was not a principal gold source).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;By 1500, the Keita dynasty retained only the Kangaba heartland&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;By the 16th century ... Manding heartland and some western provinces&quot; (Mali still held Gambian/western provinces into the 16th century).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Songhai expansion into the Niger bend by 1468&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Sonni Ali's capture of Timbuktu in 1468.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;The last mansa, Mahmud IV&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;the last powerful mansa&quot; (rulers continued after him in fragmented realms).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Mansa Musa (1312–1337)&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;c. 1312–1337&quot; (reign dates debated).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;strategic sieges, as seen at Kirina&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> removed &quot;sieges&quot; (Kirina was a field battle).</span></li></ul></details>
