<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Maratha Empire <span class="corr-n">7</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/maratha-empire">Read the profile of Maratha Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;emerged ... during the early 17th century ... challenging the waning authority of the Mughal Empire&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;mid-17th century&quot;, challenging the Deccan sultanates and in time the Mughals (Shivaji was born in 1630; Mughal power was not waning in the mid-1600s).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;The defeat at the Battle of Assaye (1803) ... marked the end of the empire's political independence&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> independence ended in 1818 with Baji Rao II's surrender and abolition of the Peshwa's office.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;After the death of Peshwa Baji Rao I in 1740, factionalism ... weakened&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> expansion continued under Balaji Baji Rao after 1740 (reaching Attock in 1758); factionalism grew alongside and after Panipat.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;sardeshmukhi (additional levy on nominally conquered territories)&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> a further tenth claimed as a hereditary right.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;The Peshwas centralized authority further ... recognized the supremacy of the Chhatrapati&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> real power passed to the Peshwa at Pune; the Chhatrapati at Satara retained formal sovereignty.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Following Shivaji's death, the empire transitioned into a confederacy&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> clarified: survived Aurangzeb's war after Sambhaji's execution (1689), confederacy under the Peshwas.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Siddhi&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;Siddis of Janjira&quot;; removed ports &quot;Rajapur and Dabhol&quot; (not securely Maratha ports) in favour of &quot;Konkan ports&quot;; removed claim that the Maratha model influenced British revenue administration (unsupported).</span></li></ul></details>
<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Maurya Empire <span class="corr-n">7</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/maurya-empire">Read the profile of Maurya Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;provincial governors (Mahamatras)&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> provinces governed by royal princes (kumaras) as viceroys, supported by mahamatras (mahamatra denoted senior officials generally).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;lessons from Hellenistic governance models&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;rooted in earlier Magadhan statecraft&quot; (no evidence the Mauryan system was modelled on Hellenistic government).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;The establishment of Pataliputra ... as the imperial capital&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Pataliputra was already the Nanda capital.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;founded in 321 BCE&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;around 321 BCE&quot;; Kalinga &quot;in 261 BCE&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;around 261 BCE&quot;; Ashoka's death &quot;in 232 BCE&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;around 232 BCE&quot; (dates approximate).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;decline occurred gradually&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;declined rapidly&quot; (the empire ended within about 50 years of Ashoka's death).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Naval forces ... supported coastal campaigns&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> river fleets (no documented Mauryan coastal campaigns).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Arthashastra presented as straightforward guide <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> noted as attributed to Chanakya but probably compiled over centuries.</span></li></ul></details>
<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Median Empire <span class="corr-n">7</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/median-empire">Read the profile of Median Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">Deioces &quot;r. c. 678–c. 655 BCE&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;traditionally r. c. 700–675 BCE&quot; (the old dates overlapped with Phraortes' reign of c. 675–653 BCE given in the same paragraph).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Cyaxares conquered Assyria (implied alone) <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> in alliance with Nabopolassar of Babylon (Nineveh fell to a Median-Babylonian coalition).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;integration of diverse populations, including ... Babylonians&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Persians, Armenians and Cappadocians (Babylonia was an ally, never under Median rule).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;defection of Median commanders during the final confrontation at Ecbatana in 550 BCE&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> per the Nabonidus Chronicle, Astyages' army mutinied when he marched against Cyrus, after which Cyrus took Ecbatana (the decisive event was not at Ecbatana).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Herodotus and subsequent cuneiform records indicate ... codified customs&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> removed (no such records; no Median legal code or archive survives).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Phraortes ... likely identified with Kashtaritu&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;possibly&quot; (identification is disputed).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">removed claim of a &quot;standing army&quot;, &quot;chariots&quot; and speculative &quot;psychological warfare ... at Nineveh&quot;.</span></li></ul></details>
<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Ming Empire <span class="corr-n">4</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/ming-empire">Read the profile of Ming Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">Zhu captured Khanbaliq and then &quot;In 1368, he declared the establishment of the Ming&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> proclaimed the Ming at Nanjing in January 1368, and captured Dadu (Khanbaliq) later that year.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Manchu forces ... invaded, culminating in the capture of Beijing in 1644&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Li Zicheng took Beijing first (April 1644, Chongzhen's suicide); the Manchus, allied with Wu Sangui, then defeated Li at Shanhai Pass and occupied the city.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;military expenditures on border defense and naval expeditions strained state finances&quot; by the late 16th century <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> border defence and the Korean war (the voyages had ended in 1433).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;enhancements to the Great Wall&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> the brick-faced walls were built mainly in the 16th century (most of the surviving Great Wall is Ming construction).</span></li></ul></details>
