<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Mutapa Empire <span class="corr-n">4</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/mutapa-empire">Read the profile of Mutapa Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;remnants of the Mutapa polity persisted into the eighteenth century&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;survived in the Zambezi valley until the Portuguese suppressed it in 1917&quot; (the rump kingdom lasted into the twentieth century)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;facilitated cultural exchanges, including the spread of Islam&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Islam &quot;made little headway among the Shona&quot; (Muslim traders were present, but there was no significant conversion)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;contributed to the monetisation of certain sectors&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;commercialised certain sectors&quot; (no monetised currency economy is attested)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;technologically advanced&quot; comparison and &quot;Kingdom of Zimbabwe&quot; softened to &quot;state centred on Great Zimbabwe&quot;; founding linked to Mutota &quot;according to tradition&quot;</span></li></ul></details>
<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Neo-Sumerian Empire <span class="corr-n">5</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/neo-sumerian-empire">Read the profile of Neo-Sumerian Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Ur-Nammu ... after expelling the Gutian rulers&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> the Gutians were expelled by Utu-hegal of Uruk; Ur-Nammu was his governor at Ur and then took power (standard reconstruction)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">military &quot;designed primarily to maintain internal stability ... rather than pursue aggressive expansion&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> served also to project power; Shulgi campaigned repeatedly in the Zagros and controlled Susa/Elam</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;king was regarded as a divine or semi-divine figure&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> kings deified in their lifetimes from Shulgi onwards</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Code of Ur-Nammu now noted as possibly issued by Shulgi (authorship debated)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">removed Anatolia as a trade source (not well attested for Ur III); replaced with Persian Gulf</span></li></ul></details>
<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Ottoman Empire <span class="corr-n">10</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Read the profile of Ottoman Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">Battle of Bapheus 1301 <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> 1302 (fought 27 July 1302)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;massive Hungarian-cast bronze cannon&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;great bronze cannon cast by the founder Urban&quot; (Urban's origin is uncertain; the guns were cast in Ottoman territory)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Mamluk conquest &quot;in 1517&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;1516–1517&quot;; caliphal transfer from the Abbasids now marked as &quot;traditionally said&quot; (the formal-transfer story is a later tradition)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">sultan as Caliph &quot;held ultimate religious authority over the Sunni world&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> claimed the title, whose political importance grew in the 18th–19th centuries; religious affairs overseen by the Şeyhülislam</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Barbarossa established dominance &quot;across the central and western Mediterranean&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;eastern and central Mediterranean&quot; (the western basin remained contested)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">1683 &quot;definitive end of Ottoman offensive capacity in Europe&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;end of Ottoman expansion in Europe&quot; (the Ottomans still won later offensives, e.g. Pruth 1711, Belgrade 1739)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Küçük Kaynarca &quot;gave Russia the right to intervene&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> provisions Russia &quot;later interpreted&quot; as such a right (contested interpretation)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Istanbul &quot;exceeding 700,000&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;several hundred thousand and perhaps as many as 700,000&quot; (estimates vary)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">millet system now noted as later formalised (the formal millet system is largely a nineteenth-century development)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Ottoman dominance as a &quot;proximate cause&quot; of the Age of Discovery <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;often cited as one of the spurs&quot; (debated)</span></li></ul></details>
<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Oyo Empire <span class="corr-n">8</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/oyo-empire">Read the profile of Oyo Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">Are-Ona-Kakanfo &quot;held hereditary responsibility&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;an appointed officer&quot; (the Kakanfo was appointed by the Alaafin, not hereditary)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Alaafin Awole's &quot;murder in 1797 by his own commanders&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> rejected and forced to take his own life around 1796 after Afonja's mutiny over the Iwere order (standard account: ritual suicide, not murder)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">New Oyo &quot;survived as a British protectorate from 1888 until its final formal dissolution in 1896&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> signed a treaty with the British in 1888 and was later absorbed into colonial Nigeria, the Alaafin's office surviving (there was no formal dissolution in 1896; the year 1896 has been removed)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Yoruba practices &quot;contributed foundationally to ... Vodou&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Yoruba (Nago) elements entered Vodou alongside predominantly Fon and Kongo roots; Haiti removed from the summary's list accordingly</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Oyo located in &quot;the western Sudan&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;northern Yorubaland&quot; (Oyo lay south of the Sahelian western Sudan)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">tributary network &quot;to the Volta corridor westward&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;across the Aja lands to the west&quot; (Oyo authority did not reach the Volta)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Basorun Gaa crisis &quot;c. 1745–1774&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;about 1754 to 1774&quot;; &quot;four successive Alaafins&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;several&quot; (accounts vary)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Oranmiyan &quot;youngest son of Oduduwa&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;a son or grandson&quot; (traditions vary)</span></li></ul></details>
<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Parthian Empire <span class="corr-n">3</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/parthian-empire">Read the profile of Parthian Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">Arsaces I &quot;seized the region of Parthia around 247 BCE&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> seized Parthia around 238 BCE, with the Arsacid era reckoned from 247 BCE (247 is the era epoch; the conquest of Parthia came c. 238)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Mithridates I &quot;established the empire as a major rival to Rome&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> first contact with Rome came under Mithridates II (Rome was not yet a factor in the region under Mithridates I)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Parni described as &quot;nomadic&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;semi-nomadic ... of the Dahae confederation&quot;</span></li></ul></details>
