en under Augustus III (exact count disputed)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">First Partition took &quot;twenty-five percent of its population&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;roughly a third&quot;</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Deluge reduced population by &quot;thirty to forty percent&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;perhaps a quarter to a third&quot;, attributed to the wars of 1648 onwards together; removed the unsupported Black Death comparison</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Kircholm &quot;barely twenty minutes&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;short and decisive&quot;; &quot;largest territorial polity in Europe&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;one of the largest&quot;; Vienna 1683 claim about saving &quot;central European Christian civilisation&quot; softened</span></li></ul></details>
<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Portuguese Empire <span class="corr-n">8</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Read the profile of Portuguese Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">da Gama's 1498 voyage placed &quot;under João II&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> under Manuel I (João II died in 1495)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Albuquerque &quot;establishing the Estado da Índia&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> the Estado da Índia was created in 1505 under Francisco de Almeida; Albuquerque built on it</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;estimated 5.8 million enslaved Africans [delivered] to Brazil&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Portuguese and Brazilian vessels carried some 5.8 million across the Atlantic, the majority to Brazil (5.8 million is the carrier total, not Brazilian arrivals)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Inquisition &quot;established in 1536 under pressure from the Spanish crown&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> at the request of King João III</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Portuguese missionaries introduced Christianity and firearms simultaneously&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> traders introduced the arquebus in 1543; the Jesuit mission began with Xavier in 1549</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Madeira sugar &quot;from the 1420s&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> settlement from the 1420s, sugar from the mid-fifteenth century</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Henry's &quot;base at Sagres&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;the Algarve&quot; (the Sagres &quot;school&quot; is largely legend); Cabral's landfall no longer called &quot;accidental&quot; (debated)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;longest-lived ... colonial empire in history&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;among the longest-lived of the European colonial empires&quot;; Goa/Malacca described as &quot;the two nodes controlling&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> key nodes, with Hormuz added; Diu enemy fleet described as Mamluk (Egyptian) and Gujarati with Calicut support</span></li></ul></details>
<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Ptolemaic Empire <span class="corr-n">2</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/ptolemaic-empire">Read the profile of Ptolemaic Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Ptolemies began to recruit local Egyptians into the army, particularly after the Battle of Raphia&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Egyptians were recruited and trained in large numbers for the Raphia campaign itself (before the battle)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Ptolemy established his capital at Alexandria&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> moved the seat of government from Memphis to Alexandria (Alexandria was founded by Alexander)</span></li></ul></details>
<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Punic Empire <span class="corr-n">11</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/punic-empire">Read the profile of Punic Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">Aristotle likened the popular assemblies to the &quot;Spartan ephors and Cretan kosmoi&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> he likened the Court of the One Hundred and Four to the ephors</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Hanno's &quot;circumnavigation of the West African coast around 500 BCE&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> a voyage down the Atlantic coast of Africa, probably in the fifth century BCE (it was not a circumnavigation)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Himilco's voyage &quot;opened tin trade routes that sustained Bronze Age metallurgy&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> sought access to the north-western tin trade (the Bronze Age had long ended by Himilco's time)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">war elephants &quot;in the empire's earlier phases&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;from the third century BCE&quot; (Carthage adopted elephants late)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">muddled naval sentence (Carthage &quot;adopted the quinquereme&quot; and Rome's corvus listed as a Carthaginian innovation) <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Rome copied a captured Carthaginian warship and devised the corvus</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Scipio Aemilianus &quot;demolished Carthage block by block over seventeen days&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> six days of street fighting, then razing; seventeen days of burning given as tradition</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Cannae &quot;greatest single-day military catastrophe in Roman history&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;one of the worst defeats&quot; (Arausio, 105 BCE, rivals it); Roman army &quot;approximately 86,000&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;perhaps 80,000&quot; in the field</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Punic Wars forced &quot;extension of Roman citizenship ... professionalisation of the legions&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> a permanent navy and provincial government (those other changes came later)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">generals &quot;appointed by ... the Council of Elders&quot;; council &quot;numbering approximately three hundred&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> generals held command separately; council size given as &quot;perhaps several hundred&quot; (both debated)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Alexander's sack &quot;drove Phoenician refugees and their accumulated wealth toward Carthage&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> some Tyrians had sent their families to Carthage beforehand</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">trade reaching &quot;the Atlantic coast of West Africa&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Atlantic coasts of Iberia and Morocco; Cato's phrase now &quot;is said to have&quot;; summary &quot;and then burn, the record&quot; removed (the libraries were given to Numidian rulers)</span></li></ul></details>
<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Qing Empire <span class="corr-n">9</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Read the profile of Qing Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Eight Banners system around 1601&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> four banners in 1601, expanded to eight by 1615</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Yongzheng reforms &quot;consolidating the Ming taxation system into a simplified land-and-poll tax levied in silver&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> merged the poll tax into the land tax and regularised surcharges, following Kangxi's 1712 freeze of the poll-tax quota</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">silver imports &quot;primarily ... via Spanish trade&quot; whose disruption caused deflation <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> silver came largely from the Americas, and the outflow was driven chiefly by rising opium imports</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Sino-Japanese War &quot;forced the evacuation of Korea&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> recognition of Korean independence</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Literary Inquisition presented as Qianlong's alone <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> inquisitions peaked under Qianlong (linked to the Siku Quanshu) but existed earlier</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Dzungar deaths &quot;perhaps 600,000&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;perhaps several hundred thousand&quot; (estimates vary); campaign dated 1755–1758 (fighting continued until 1758); greatest extent given as a range of 13 to 14.7 million km² (estimates vary)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;approximately thirty percent of world industrial output&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;around a third of the world's manufactured goods&quot;, marked as an estimate; Taiping &quot;the most destructive civil war in human history&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;among the most destructive&quot;</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;ending 2,132 years of continuous imperial rule&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;more than two thousand years of imperial rule&quot; (imperial China was not continuously unified)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Impact claim that the PRC &quot;has defended [the borders] as historically inalienable ever since ... direct ancestor of contemporary Chinese territorial claims and border disputes&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> neutral statement that the Qing framework underpinned the modern Chinese state, noting Outer Mongolia's separation (removes modern political commentary)</span></li></ul></details>
<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Rashidun Caliphate <span class="corr-n">8</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Read the profile of Rashidun Caliphate →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">Uthman's killing was &quot;the first murder of a caliph in Islamic history&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> the first murder of a caliph by Muslim rebels (Umar had already been assassinated in 644 CE; this is now stated)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Khalid ibn al-Walid's successor Saad ibn Abi Waqqas&quot; at Qadisiyyah <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Saad ibn Abi Waqqas (he did not succeed Khalid, who had transferred to Syria)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Arab authority &quot;from the Atlantic approaches of Libya&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;from Libya&quot; (Libya has no Atlantic coast; the Atlantic was reached only under the Umayyads)</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;destroyed two ancient empires&quot; (summary) <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> destroyed one (the Sasanian) and stripped another (the Byzantine) of its richest provinces, consistent with the impact section</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;virtually the entire Arabian Peninsula rose&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;much of Arabia&quot;; &quot;militarily invincible&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> faced no neighbouring power capable of threatening it</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Uthman killed by &quot;disaffected Egyptian soldiers&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> rebels, many of them from Egypt; reading the Quran marked as tradition</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Yarmuk command attributed solely to Khalid <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Abu Ubayda and Khalid; dust storm marked as tradition; Arab armies described as &quot;predominantly infantry&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> infantry and light cavalry using camels for movement</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;the dhimmi framework ... compared favourably with&quot; Byzantine and Sasanian practice <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> comparative judgement removed (contested)</span></li></ul></details>
<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Roman Empire <span class="corr-n">8</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/roman-empire">Read the profile of Roman Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;a culture that had executed a king seven centuries earlier&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;had expelled its last king, Tarquinius Superbus, nearly five centuries earlier&quot; (Tarquin was expelled, not executed, c. 509 BCE).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;elimination of internal tariffs&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;generally modest customs dues (portoria) levied at provincial boundaries&quot; (internal customs duties existed, and the text itself mentions customs duties).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">legions &quot;later rebuilt to 33 after the Teutoburg disaster&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> 25 after Teutoburg, rising gradually to around 33 by Septimius Severus.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;systematic codification begun under Augustus&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> jurisprudence fostered from Augustus onwards, consolidated by Hadrian's Perpetual Edict (no systematic codification under Augustus).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Diocletian as &quot;dominus et deus&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> addressed as dominus (lord) (the &quot;dominus et deus&quot; formula is chiefly associated with Domitian and Aurelian).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Roman coinage found in &quot;Vietnam and Nigeria&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;southern India and the Mekong Delta&quot; (Nigerian finds are not well established); trade link to China made &quot;indirectly&quot;.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;more than fifty men claimed the imperial title&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;some two dozen recognised emperors and many more usurpers&quot;.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Adrianople &quot;by Visigothic forces&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;Gothic forces&quot; (the Goths at Adrianople included Tervingi and Greuthungi; &quot;Visigoths&quot; is anachronistic for 378).</span></li></ul></details>
<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Rozvi Empire <span class="corr-n">6</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/rozvi-empire">Read the profile of Rozvi Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">Mfecane forces were &quot;musket-equipped Nguni raiding forces&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Nguni forces organised in regiments using close-combat stabbing-spear tactics (Mfecane-era Nguni armies were not musket-armed).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;the Mutapa's Qizilbash-style tributary levies&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;the Mutapa's tributary levies&quot; (Qizilbash are Safavid Turkmen; anachronistic and irrelevant).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Khami listed as a Rozvi construction <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Khami identified as the earlier Torwa capital which the Rozvi inherited; Rozvi associated with Danangombe and Naletale. Added that Dombo overran the Torwa state of Butua.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">post-expulsion trade &quot;redirected away from Portuguese-dominated channels ... toward Swahili coastal merchants operating through Quelimane and Sofala&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> trade continued with Portuguese and Afro-Portuguese merchants but confined to licensed fairs (Zumbo, Masekesa) on Rozvi terms; Quelimane and Sofala were Portuguese-held, not Swahili-run.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">decline said the &quot;last Changamire, Chirisamhuru II&quot; was killed while the same ruler name was given a 76-year reign ending 1788 (internally inconsistent and unattested) <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> removed the 76-year reign (c. 1712–1788) and the 1788 death; the ruler killed by Nyamazana's forces is &quot;traditionally named as Chirisamhuru&quot;.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Zwangendaba's Ngoni &quot;from approximately 1826&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;in the early 1830s&quot;; removed &quot;Ngwana's forces&quot; (not securely attested).</span></li></ul></details>
<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Russian Empire <span class="corr-n">7</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Read the profile of Russian Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">summary &quot;the largest contiguous territorial state in human history&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;one of the largest contiguous empires in history&quot; (the Mongol Empire was larger); &quot;landlocked Muscovite principality&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;the Muscovite state&quot; (Muscovy had Arctic access via Arkhangelsk).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;the First Russo-Turkish War (1768–1774)&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;the Russo-Turkish Wars of 1768–1774 and 1787–1792&quot; (there were several earlier Russo-Turkish wars).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">governorates &quot;each administered by a governor-general&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> administered by governors, with governors-general over groups of provinces.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;conquest of the Siberian khanates in the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;the conquest of the Khanate of Sibir in the late sixteenth century&quot;.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Narva &quot;8,000 Swedish troops ... Russian army of 40,000&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;roughly 10,000 ... some 35,000 to 40,000&quot; (usual estimates).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Rasputin's influence &quot;on questions of military appointments&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;over ministerial appointments&quot;.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;without a single loyal military unit prepared to defend the dynasty&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> his generals urged abdication (hyperbole removed). Congress of Vienna dated 1814–1815.</span></li></ul></details>
<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Safavid Empire <span class="corr-n">7</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/safavid-empire">Read the profile of Safavid Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">Ghilzai revolt under Mir Wais &quot;from 1704 onward&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;in 1709&quot; (Mir Wais killed the Safavid governor Gurgin Khan in 1709).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;grand vizier (vakil or sadr)&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> grand vizier (later etemad al-dowleh) for civil affairs, with the sadr as head of the religious establishment (the sadr was not the grand vizier).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Chaldiran &quot;cost the Safavids Tabriz&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Tabriz was only briefly occupied; Diyarbakir and much of eastern Anatolia were lost permanently.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">classical carpets (Vienna, Milan, Boston) produced &quot;under Shah Abbas I&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> court workshops of the sixteenth and early seventeenth centuries (the Ardabil and the Vienna/Milan hunting carpets date from Tahmasp I's reign); Boston dropped, London added.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Sultan Husayn &quot;delegated effective power to the clerical establishment under Majlesi&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Majlesi influential early in the reign (he died in 1699, a decade before the Afghan revolt).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Armenians moved &quot;from the Ararat region&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> from Julfa on the Aras river.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Safaviyya prestige &quot;for over a century&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;some two centuries&quot;; fragmentation &quot;since the Mongol invasions of the thirteenth century&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;since the break-up of the Ilkhanate in the fourteenth century&quot;; maximum extent &quot;by approximately 1620&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;in the 1620s&quot; (Baghdad fell in 1624).</span></li></ul></details>
<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Sasanian Empire <span class="corr-n">8</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Read the profile of Sasanian Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;the ancient Achaemenid ceremonial capital of Ctesiphon&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;the former Parthian capital of Ctesiphon&quot; (Ctesiphon was a Parthian, not Achaemenid, capital); coronation/entry dated to around 226.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">treaty of 363 ceded &quot;Nisibis, Singara, and fifteen eastern provinces&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;Nisibis, Singara, five provinces beyond the Tigris and fifteen fortresses&quot;; treaty concluded with Jovian.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Khosrow I's &quot;four regional commands (marzban)&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> four regional generals (spahbed); marzban were frontier governors.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Naqsh-e Rostam relief &quot;showing Valerian kneeling&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Valerian appears as captive beside the kneeling Philip the Arab.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">land tax &quot;xwastak&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> harag; poll tax &quot;on non-Zoroastrian subject populations&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> gazidag levied on commoners, with nobles, priests, soldiers and scribes exempt.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Khosrow II's armies reached Constantinople &quot;between 604 and 619&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Egypt conquered by around 621, Bosphorus reached and siege of 626; extent &quot;exceeding the empire's traditional maximum&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> rivalling the Achaemenid empire.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Gundishapur as &quot;the most important intellectual institution of the pre-Islamic world&quot; that hosted the Athenian philosophers <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> philosophers came to Khosrow's court; Gundishapur's academy reputation probably exaggerated.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">four estates' commoners now correctly split into wastaryoshan (peasants) and hutukhshan (artisans); &quot;Persian displaced Arabic&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> New Persian &quot;rivalled&quot; Arabic.</span></li></ul></details>
<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Seleucid Empire <span class="corr-n">7</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Read the profile of Seleucid Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">Battle of Panium &quot;Antiochus's cavalry under Scopas&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Seleucid cavalry led by Antiochus III's son Antiochus; Scopas was the Aetolian commander of the defeated Ptolemaic army.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Battle of Gaza (312 BCE) won by &quot;the coalition of Ptolemy, Cassander and Lysimachus&quot; against Antigonus <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> won by Ptolemy and Seleucus against Antigonus's son Demetrius.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Seleucia-on-Tigris founded &quot;near the ruins of Babylon&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> on the Tigris north of Babylon.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">decline &quot;spanning nearly 150 years&quot; (188–63 BCE) <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;some 125 years&quot;.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Seleucus I founded &quot;between nine and sixteen cities named Seleucia&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> per Appian, nine named Seleucia and sixteen named Antioch.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Antiochus III's 212–205 campaign &quot;decade-long&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;seven-year&quot;; &quot;Media Atropatene&quot; replaced by Armenia (the anabasis began with Armenia); Seleucus &quot;senior cavalry commander ... commander of the elite Companion infantry (hypaspists)&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> commanded the royal hypaspists under Alexander and the Companion cavalry under Perdiccas.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Demetrius II's capture placed in 138 BCE (the text implied it coincided with the fall of Seleucia in 141). Fifth Syrian War dated 202–195 BCE (formal peace 195), Panium c. 200 BCE.</span></li></ul></details>
<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Seljuk Empire <span class="corr-n">6</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Read the profile of Seljuk Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Malik Shah's death in 1092, followed within weeks by Nizam al-Mulk's assassination&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Nizam al-Mulk was assassinated first (October 1092), followed within weeks by Malik Shah's death (November 1092).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Nizamiyya madrasas &quot;institutionalised Shafi'i and Hanafi jurisprudence&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Shafi'i jurisprudence and Ash'ari theology (the Nizamiyyas were Shafi'i foundations).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Romanos IV's capture &quot;unprecedented in Byzantine history&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;the first time a Byzantine emperor had been taken prisoner by a Muslim ruler&quot;.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Tughril and Chaghri &quot;consolidated tribal strength in Transoxiana through the 1030s&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> led their tribesmen into Khorasan in the 1030s (Merv and Nishapur are in Khorasan).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Malik Shah reached &quot;the borders of China&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> received the submission of the Karakhanids of Samarkand and Kashgar, &quot;to the borders of the Chinese world&quot;.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Ghurids pressing from the east (the Ghurids rose after the Great Seljuk heyday) <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;new powers pressed from the east&quot;; Qara Khitai &quot;Buddhist Sinicised&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;Sinicised Khitan&quot;.</span></li></ul></details>
