al-Latif.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Ulugh Beg's star tables &quot;used by European astronomers for a century&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> later studied in Europe, published at Oxford in 1665; Delhi massacre figure marked &quot;reportedly&quot;; Zafarnama claim attributed to court historians generally.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">garbled &quot;Timur's descent from the destruction of most of the cities&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> founder's reputation as destroyer of the cities his heirs governed.</span></li></ul></details>
<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Tokugawa Shogunate <span class="corr-n">7</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Read the profile of Tokugawa Shogunate →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">sakoku &quot;restricted foreign trade to a single Dutch trading post at Dejima&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Dutch (from 1641) and Chinese traded at Nagasaki; Korea, Ryukyu and Ainu contacts ran through Tsushima, Satsuma and Matsumae.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">tea ceremony and garden design &quot;reached their classical forms under Tokugawa patronage&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> older arts further refined and disseminated (the tea ceremony's classical form predates the Tokugawa, e.g. Sen no Rikyu).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;haiku poetry form developed by Matsuo Basho&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;the hokku verse that Matsuo Basho raised to a high art&quot;.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;fifteen generations&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> fifteen shoguns.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Boshin War narrated before Yoshinobu's resignation <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> reordered chronologically (resignation Nov 1867, restoration proclaimed 3 Jan 1868, then the Boshin War); &quot;most peaceable transition&quot; softened given the war.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Sekigahara &quot;the largest battle ever fought on Japanese soil&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;one of the largest&quot;; &quot;defeating the Toyotomi coalition&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> western coalition under Ishida Mitsunari acting in the name of Hideyoshi's heir; Ieyasu as &quot;subordinate&quot; of Nobunaga <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> ally of Nobunaga, vassal of Hideyoshi.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;burakumin&quot; (a modern term) <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> eta and hinin; Edo &quot;village&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> modest castle town; Edo one million &quot;by 1700&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;by the early eighteenth century&quot;; ukiyo-e packing-material story marked as anecdotal; Black Ships described as a squadron including steam frigates.</span></li></ul></details>
<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Umayyad Caliphate <span class="corr-n">7</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Read the profile of Umayyad Caliphate →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">Yarmuk (636) and al-Qadisiyya presented as Umayyad victories <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> attributed to the Rashidun period that preceded the dynasty (Muawiyah's role as governor of Syria noted).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Egypt, Syria, Iraq and Persia &quot;became predominantly Muslim within two to three generations&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Muslims probably became a majority only over the following centuries.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Muawiyah &quot;transferred its capital from Medina to Damascus&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> made Damascus the seat in place of Ali's base at Kufa and the earlier capital of Medina.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Abbasids &quot;transferred the caliphate to a new capital at Baghdad&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> shifted the centre to Iraq, where al-Mansur founded Baghdad in 762.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;a single survivor, Abd al-Rahman I&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;the most famous survivor&quot;; Emirate of Córdoba dated 756.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">jizya/conversion tension &quot;under the Abbasids&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> the tension arose under the Umayyads themselves (al-Hajjaj, Umar II's reforms).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Karbala &quot;permanently divided the Muslim community into Sunni and Shia&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;deepened the division&quot;; &quot;the most rapid territorial expansion in history&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;one of the most rapid&quot;; &quot;exceeded Alexander&quot; softened; &quot;largest empire yet assembled&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;one of the largest&quot;; Berber revolt causes restated (taxation, slave levies, unequal treatment) rather than land division; &quot;Christian and Greek-speaking&quot; applied only to former Byzantine lands; &quot;fourteen centuries&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;thirteen centuries&quot;; &quot;most influential language after Latin&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;one of the great vehicles of pre-modern learning&quot;.</span></li></ul></details>
<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Wadai Empire <span class="corr-n">8</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/wadai-empire">Read the profile of Wadai Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;the physical boundary of modern Chad substantially corresponds to the territorial extent of the Wadai Empire&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Wadai's heartland forms the Ouaddaï region of eastern Chad (Wadai covered only eastern Chad).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Sultan Doud Murra (r. 1898–1901, 1903–1909)&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Muhammad Dud Murra (r. 1902–1909), who resisted until his surrender in 1911.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Battle of Abéché in June 1909 — French colonial force under Colonel Moll&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> French column captured Abéché in June 1909 and installed Adam Asil (Moll's name removed; he was killed in 1910 in a different engagement).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;the French Mandate formally incorporated Wadai into French Equatorial Africa&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> absorbed into the colony of Chad within French Equatorial Africa (there was no mandate); sultanate abolished 1912.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;after their conquest of Bornu in 1900&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Rabih's defeat and death at Kousséri, April 1900.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Wadai's position on the &quot;forty-day road (Darb al-Arba'in) to Asyut&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Wadai's main artery was the Kufra–Benghazi route opened under Sabun (the Darb al-Arba'in was Darfur's route).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;declining Kanem-Bornu Empire&quot; in the early 1600