<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Xiongnu Empire <span class="corr-n">6</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/xiongnu-empire">Read the profile of Xiongnu Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">Baideng &quot;surrounded by Xiongnu cavalry with 300,000 men&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Chinese sources number the force at 400,000 horsemen (Shiji); escape was by bribing the chanyu's consort, not open negotiation.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Huhanye called &quot;the Southern Xiongnu chanyu&quot; in 51 BCE <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;the chanyu Huhanye&quot; (the Southern/Northern division dates from 48 CE); added Zhizhi's death in 36 BCE.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Yuezhi &quot;expelled ... by Modu's campaigns around 176 BCE&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> defeated by Modu around 176 BCE, finally driven out under Laoshang.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Xiongnu law &quot;death for minor offences&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Shiji's account: death for drawing a blade more than a foot, household confiscation for theft, beating for lesser offences.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">summary &quot;population pressures ultimately contributed to the fall of both the Han Dynasty and the Western Roman Empire&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> migrations reshaped China, Central Asia and &quot;perhaps indirectly&quot; the late Roman world (Han fall link unsupported; Hun-Xiongnu link debated). Impact: Huns &quot;set in motion the population movements that destroyed&quot; Rome softened to &quot;contributed to the crisis&quot;.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;noyons&quot; as a term for burial mounds <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> named sites Noin-Ula (Noyon Uul) and Gol Mod; &quot;Bactrian gold work&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> textiles from Central Asia and the Hellenistic world; Jurchen removed from steppe-empire list (not a steppe confederation); &quot;120s–110s BCE&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> 129–119 BCE.</span></li></ul></details>
<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Yuan Dynasty <span class="corr-n">8</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/yuan-dynasty">Read the profile of Yuan Dynasty →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;his cousin Ariq Böke&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> his younger brother Ariq Böke; &quot;his nephew Kaidu&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> his kinsman Kaidu (a grandson of Ögedei).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Kublai &quot;reinstated (in modified form) the civil service examinations&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> examinations were not restored until 1315 under Renzong, with quotas favouring Mongols and Semu.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;the Dalai Lama institution's relationship with Chinese imperial governance&quot; (the Dalai Lama title dates from 1578) <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Yuan patron-preceptor relationship with the Sakya hierarchs.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Yuan as &quot;the decisive moment in the spread of Tibetan Buddhism into Mongolia&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> laid foundations; mass conversion of Mongolia came in the sixteenth century.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;the most populous empire in world history&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> &quot;one of the most populous states in the world&quot;.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Black Death in China &quot;1330s–1350s ... 30 to 60 percent mortality&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> severe epidemics recorded; identification with the Black Death debated (unsupported mortality figures removed).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Northern Yuan existence &quot;until the early fifteenth century&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> later Mongol khans long continued to claim the Yuan inheritance.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">compass removed from summary list of East-to-West transmissions (it reached Europe before the Mongol era); mechanical clock and European glassmaking removed from exchange claims (unsupported); Phagspa &quot;never successfully adopted&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> used officially on seals, coins, passes but never displaced other scripts; chao &quot;exclusive legal tender&quot; softened.</span></li></ul></details>
<details class="corr-prof"><summary>Zand Empire <span class="corr-n">8</span></summary><p><a href="/empire/zand-empire">Read the profile of Zand Empire →</a></p><ul class="corr-list corr-short"><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;twelve rulers in twenty-one years of civil war&quot; / &quot;twelve different rulers&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> a rapid succession of rulers across fifteen years (1779–1794).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;capturing Shiraz in 1791, losing it&quot; and &quot;The Qajar conquest of Shiraz the following year [1795] completed the dissolution&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Shiraz was betrayed to the Qajars by Haji Ebrahim Khan Kalantar in 1791; dissolution completed with Lotf Ali Khan's capture in 1794.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Lotf Ali Khan &quot;captured ... at the siege of Kerman&quot;, &quot;betrayed by the city's governor&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> after Kerman fell he fled and was betrayed at Bam.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;Abu al-Fath Khan was immediately deposed by his uncle Ali Murad Khan&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Zaki Khan seized power in the names of Abol-Fath and Mohammad Ali; Ali Morad came later.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Vakil complex &quot;designated a UNESCO World Heritage Site&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> removed (not a World Heritage listing).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">European companies at &quot;Bushire (Bandar Rig)&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> British at Bushehr (grant of 1763); Dutch on Kharg Island until 1766 (Bandar Rig is a different port).</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">&quot;expelled the Ottomans from Basra&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> captured Basra (1776), abandoned after Karim Khan's death; &quot;the Lak branch of the Zand confederation&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> the Zand, a Lak tribe; &quot;Safavid or Chinggisid dynastic credentials&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> Safavid lineage; Agha Mohammad castrated &quot;on the orders of a rival&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> by Adel Shah; Kerman blinding &quot;tens of thousands&quot; <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only"> became </span> reportedly some 20,000.</span></li><li><span class="corr-was">Karim Khan's control of &quot;most of Iran&quot; qualified: Khorasan remained under the Afsharid Shahrokh; &quot;restored food production to pre-Nader Shah levels within a generation&quot; softened.</span></li></ul></details></section>
